1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,097
A supplier calls at nine in the morning:
one lot of kettles can overheat.

2
00:00:04,097 --> 00:00:08,345
Somewhere inside eight thousand orders are
the few dozen that actually shipped

3
00:00:08,345 --> 00:00:12,465
that lot — next to a nearly identical lot
number that is perfectly

4
00:00:12,465 --> 00:00:13,299
safe.

5
00:00:13,299 --> 00:00:16,389
Miss one, and a hazard stays in somebody's
kitchen.

6
00:00:16,389 --> 00:00:18,206
Over-recall, and you alarm

7
00:00:18,206 --> 00:00:20,139
thousands of customers for nothing.

8
00:00:20,139 --> 00:00:22,113
Today this is CSV exports and a

9
00:00:22,113 --> 00:00:25,509
spreadsheet war room, at the worst
possible hour.

10
00:00:25,509 --> 00:00:28,669
An assistant that only sees the screen
doesn't help.

11
00:00:28,669 --> 00:00:30,567
It guesses which control to

12
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:32,836
click, and nobody can audit a guess.

13
00:00:32,836 --> 00:00:36,192
So this page publishes its real operations
instead.

14
00:00:36,192 --> 00:00:38,391
WebMCP turns the recall

15
00:00:38,391 --> 00:00:43,843
itself — trace, classify, stage — into
tools the browser hands to the agent.

16
00:00:43,843 --> 00:00:46,366
Fresh Codex session, site tools on.

17
00:00:46,366 --> 00:00:49,491
Nine tools, registered once by the page,
in

18
00:00:49,491 --> 00:00:51,829
the order a recall actually happens.

19
00:00:51,829 --> 00:00:55,567
One prompt, in the language an operations
lead would actually use.

20
00:00:55,567 --> 00:00:57,951
Watch the page, not the transcript.

21
00:00:57,951 --> 00:01:00,880
Every call commits to the visible
interface

22
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:05,984
before the agent gets its answer, so the
human and the model share one source of

23
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:06,713
truth.

24
00:01:06,713 --> 00:01:09,560
Exact-lot equality does the dangerous
part.

25
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:12,199
Thirty-seven recipients, and every

26
00:01:12,199 --> 00:01:17,824
lookalike order deliberately held out —
counted on screen as proof, not as a

27
00:01:17,824 --> 00:01:18,692
promise.

28
00:01:18,692 --> 00:01:23,981
Every match then gets exactly one
obligation: hold it in the warehouse,

29
00:01:23,981 --> 00:01:27,407
intercept it in transit, or call the
person who already has it.

30
00:01:27,407 --> 00:01:32,801
Quarantines, carrier intercepts, customer
letters — all drafted, none released.

31
00:01:32,801 --> 00:01:35,116
Durable actions, still zero.

32
00:01:35,116 --> 00:01:36,956
So I tell it to just send everything.

33
00:01:36,956 --> 00:01:38,449
There is nothing for it to call.

34
00:01:38,449 --> 00:01:42,072
No confirm tool, no commit tool, no send
tool —

35
00:01:42,072 --> 00:01:46,875
the consequential half of this workflow
was never exposed as an API.

36
00:01:46,875 --> 00:01:47,535
That

37
00:01:47,535 --> 00:01:51,597
guardrail is the shape of the interface,
not a sentence in a prompt.

38
00:01:51,597 --> 00:01:53,287
The last move stays human.

39
00:01:53,287 --> 00:01:56,481
I read the evidence, I take the decision,
and the

40
00:01:56,481 --> 00:01:59,815
receipt records that it came from the
visible review control.

41
00:01:59,815 --> 00:02:02,315
Now the agent reads back what was
approved.

42
00:02:02,315 --> 00:02:04,595
The only tool left after the commit

43
00:02:04,595 --> 00:02:05,602
is read-only.

44
00:02:05,602 --> 00:02:08,646
The model does the precise, exhausting
work.

45
00:02:08,646 --> 00:02:10,718
The page keeps every step

46
00:02:10,718 --> 00:02:11,562
reviewable.

47
00:02:11,562 --> 00:02:14,919
And the irreversible click still belongs
to a person.

48
00:02:14,919 --> 00:02:15,937
Give the agent

49
00:02:15,937 --> 00:02:18,403
hands — keep the last decision human.
